{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004606",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 6 candidates are direct matches by trait_efo (age-related macular degeneration), but they differ in endpoint fidelity and evidentiary strength. PGS004606 has the cleanest phenotype alignment (phenotyping_reported: “Age-related macular degeneration”), comes from a disease-focused GWAS/meta-analysis context (IAMDGC; Nat Genet 2024), and provides interpretable discrimination evidence with basic covariates (age, sex, PCs) including a reported AUROC=0.71 and OR per SD=1.76 in a large validation sample (n=163,011, EUR). Competing options are weaker: PGS001834/PGS002041 are from a UKB portability framework and only show small partial-r (~0.016–0.018) rather than directly comparable PRS discrimination; PGS002269 has a phenotype mismatch (retinal layer thickness) despite AMD labeling; PGS004607 is trained on AFR GWAS but evaluated in EUR with lower AUROC (0.65) and lower OR (1.48); PGS004952 reports very high AUROC but is for late AMD with a small validation size (n=1,232) and has a likely metric-format inconsistency (AUROC listed as 84.2 in classification_metrics), making its performance evidence less reliable/comparable from the visible metadata. Overall, PGS004606 is best supported, but confidence is limited because PRS-only (covariate-free) performance is not provided and reported AUCs appear to be full-model metrics rather than explicitly PGS-only."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001268",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 9 candidates are direct matches to ankylosing spondylitis by trait_efo, with most also having endpoint-faithful phenotyping_reported. Among the best-supported options, PGS001268 (snpnet; UKB) provides the strongest comparable European validation package with explicitly reported PRS-only and full-model metrics (PGS-only AUROC=0.7346; full-model AUROC=0.74878 with CI; PGS R2(no covariates)=0.10232; incremental AUROC=0.12686) and a large validation sample (n=67,425), edging PGS001267 on performance. However, confidence is limited because the snpnet models come from a pan-trait UKB framework paper (per publication title), which domain guidance flags as lower-prior than disease-focused scores; and the alternative UKB portability scores (PGS002089/PGS001876) lack directly comparable AUROC/R2 (only partial-r), while the Taiwanese EAS study models (PGS003420-PGS003424) report only full-model AUROC with unknown covariates and small validation (n=1,298), making cross-candidate comparability and ancestry-general deployment unclear. Thus a direct match exists, but evidence is not strong enough to claim a clearly high-quality winner across study archetypes and metric comparability."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005254","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Among the 8 returned models, the direct-match candidates for the target trait (aortic stenosis) are PGS005252 and PGS005254 (trait_reported=aortic stenosis; phenotyping_reported=incident aortic stenosis). PGS005255 and PGS005256 are aortic valve function measurements (mean pressure gradient / peak aortic velocity) rather than the disease itself, so they are weaker direct matches; the hypertrophic cardiomyopathy models are not direct matches.

Between the two disease-endpoint candidates, PGS005254 is preferred because it uses only basic demographic/PC covariates (sex, age, age^2, PCs 1–5), making its reported association more comparable to a PRS contribution, and it reports an explicit HR with CI (HR=1.64, 95% CI 1.50–1.78) in a substantial validation sample (n=244,450). By contrast, PGS005252 reports a high full-model C-index (0.87) but includes multiple clinical comorbidities and risk factors (e.g., diabetes, hypertension, CAD, hyperlipidemia, BMI, smoking, renal failure), which the provided domain guidance flags as likely to inflate full-model discrimination and makes the metric less informative about PRS quality; it also lacks PRS-only or incremental metrics.

Limitation: neither aortic stenosis score provides PRS-only AUROC/R² or incremental AUROC in the visible metadata, so the ranking relies on endpoint fidelity plus covariate comparability and effect-size support rather than directly comparable PRS discrimination metrics, lowering confidence."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000452",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 20 candidates are direct matches on concept (`trait_efo` = basal cell carcinoma). For generic BCC, several Cancer PRSweb models (PGS000445–PGS000459) have clean phenotype alignment (`phenotyping_reported`: Basal cell carcinoma), basic covariates (age/sex/batch/PCs), and comparable EUR evaluation. Within this coherent family, PGS000452 has the strongest reported discrimination among models with available AUROC (full-model AUROC 0.632, 95% CI 0.616–0.647) and the largest reported PRS-effect signal (OR per SD 1.663, CI 1.57–1.761; top 1% vs rest OR 3.61), with a reasonable validation size (n=11,322). A key limitation is that reported AUROCs here appear to be full-model (not explicitly PRS-only), so cross-model comparisons are imperfect; additionally, a different study (PGS000119) reports a slightly higher full-model AUROC (0.64) but with smaller validation (n=20,468) and fewer variants, while newer 2023/2025 scores (PGS003416/PGS003417/PGS005207) lack AUROC/R2 in the provided context, preventing a stronger evidence-based preference. Hence moderate confidence despite a best-supported choice."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS003725",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates for coronary artery disease are present in abundance (85). Among the visible set, PGS003725 (Nat Med 2023; LDpred2; 1,296,172 variants) has strong endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: “Coronary artery disease”), large validation (n=308,264), and a modern multi-ancestry, multi-biobank development/validation context (AGENT2D, BBJ, CARDIoGRAMplusC4D, FinnGen, GBMI, MVP, UKB), with supportive effect sizes reported (HR=1.75; OR=2.14). However, key PRS-comparable performance metrics (PGS-only AUROC/R2 or incremental metrics) are not provided for this candidate (and several others), while some older scores report high full-model AUROC but with potential non-comparability (e.g., PGS001780 full-model AUROC=0.913) and/or restricted endpoints (e.g., PGS000116 incident CAD under age 55 with QRISK3 covariate packaging). Given the missing PRS-only discrimination metrics for the leading modern multi-cohort candidates, selection is best-supported but not definitive, lowering the outcome to DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL and confidence to Moderate."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS003725",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates are present (many models map to EFO coronary artery disease). Among the visible set, PGS003725 is preferred because it has strong endpoint fidelity (trait_reported and phenotyping_reported are both coronary artery disease) and comes from a recent disease-focused, multi-ancestry PRS study (Nat Med 2023) with large validation support (n=308,264) and strong per-SD effect sizes (HR=1.75; OR=2.14), suggesting meaningful PRS signal without obvious risk-calculator packaging. However, confidence is not High because key PRS-comparable discrimination metrics (PGS-only AUROC/R2, incremental AUROC) are missing for PGS003725, and some competitors report high full-model AUROC (e.g., PGS000018 AUROC=0.89; PGS001780 AUROC=0.913), but these are not directly comparable PRS-only metrics and may be covariate-driven; the absence of comparable metrics across models limits a definitive best-choice claim."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001789",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match gout PRS candidates are present. PGS001789 (PRS-CS-auto; Cell Genom 2022) is preferred because it uses a direct gout endpoint with basic demographic/PC covariates and is developed/validated in a large multi-biobank context with very large validation (n=359,345), which is supportive for robustness/transportability compared with UKB-only pan-trait/sparse framework scores (e.g., PGS001248/PGS001249) and small-sample/outpatient-ascertainment models (e.g., PGS000199). However, the decision remains sub-optimal because comparable PRS-isolated performance is inconsistently available across candidates: many report only full-model AUROC (often with covariates) or effect sizes, limiting clean cross-model comparison; additionally, some newer integrative models (e.g., PGS004768) report incremental R2 but have much smaller validation (n=9,462), making it unclear whether they outperform in a comparable setting."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001320",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match hypertension PRS candidates are present (e.g., PGS000706, PGS001320, PGS001838, PGS002047, PGS004234, PGS004236, PGS004934). Among the directly aligned models, PGS001320 has clean phenotype alignment (trait_efo/phenotyping_reported both 'Hypertension'), reports explicit PRS-only performance (PGS AUROC (no covariates)=0.62908; PGS R2 (no covariates)=0.06493) plus incremental AUROC (0.04424), and is trained/validated in UKB with standard/basic covariates (age, sex, array, PCs), avoiding obvious clinical risk-wrapper packaging. However, confidence is not high because multiple other direct-match candidates exist with potentially stronger headline full-model discrimination (e.g., PGS004234/PGS004236 AUROC ~0.763–0.764) but with less comparable/less informative PRS-only metrics and/or more complex multi-ethnic prevalence/progression framing; additionally, the selected model comes from a pan-trait UKB framework ('813 traits' snpnet family), which domain guidance suggests should be down-weighted when cleaner disease-focused multi-cohort alternatives exist, but the context provided does not include a clearly superior hypertension-specific multi-cohort model with directly comparable PRS-only metrics to justify replacing PGS001320."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS003578",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates for major depressive disorder are present (24 total). Several are endpoint-faithful (phenotyping_reported explicitly \"Major Depressive Disorder\" / \"Major depressive disorder\" / \"Incident MDD\"). Among these, PGS003578 (\"Major Depressive Disorder (Lifetime)\") has the highest reported variance explained in European validation within the coherent 2023-04-12 UKB PRSice-2 study family (R²=0.0257; n=42,250; EUR validation), edging similar family members (e.g., PGS003580 R²=0.0254; PGS003576 R²=0.0241). Competing candidates either have weaker direct endpoint fidelity (e.g., PGS000767 validates putamen volume; PGS000907 validates antidepressant side-effect muscle pain; PGS002789 validates internalising behavior) or provide less comparable/credible performance signals for generic MDD risk ranking (e.g., PGS000138/145 report modest full-model AUROC; PGS004885 reports C-index for incident MDD but uses a very small training sample n=404 and a different time-to-event framing). Remaining limitation: most candidates lack clearly PRS-only metrics and covariate reporting is sometimes unknown, and the small R² differences within the UKB family make the top choice only moderately separable from close alternatives; thus confidence is Moderate and the overall direct-match recommendation is sub-optimal rather than clearly high-quality."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004760",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "There are multiple direct-match candidates for major depressive disorder, but several have endpoint-fidelity issues or non-comparable/implausible performance reporting, making a clearly best choice difficult. PGS004760 (Depression; trait_efo=major depressive disorder) uses a modern integrative method (PRSmixPlus), has basic demographic/PC covariates (age, sex, PC1-10), and reports an incremental R2=0.024 (CI 0.018–0.03) in European evaluation (n=9,462), which is directly interpretable as added predictive contribution beyond covariates and is in the same range as other clean MDD endpoints (e.g., PRSice-2 MDD models with R2 ~0.02–0.026). Competing models are limited by: (i) endpoint mismatch (e.g., PGS000767 evaluates putamen volume; PGS000907 evaluates muscle pain in fluoxetine takers; PGS002789/PGS003442 evaluate cognition/internalising-related phenotypes), (ii) older C+T UKB-only scores with modest full-model AUROC/R2 (PGS000138/139/145) and no PRS-only AUROC, or (iii) metrics that are not comparable/suspiciously high for a PRS (PGS000907 reports Nagelkerke R2*100=0.77) or are time-to-event packaged (PGS004885 reports C-index=0.62 for incident MDD). Remaining limitation: PGS004760’s validation size is modest compared with some other direct MDD-status evaluations (e.g., PGS000193 n=92,957; PGS003576-85 n=42,250), and cross-model comparability is constrained because many candidates lack PRS-only AUROC/R2 or report different metric types."
}

