  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "There are multiple direct-match candidates for major depressive disorder, but many have endpoint-fidelity issues or non-comparable/unclear metrics, making the best choice not unequivocal. Several models have phenotyping_reported directly as MDD/depression (e.g., PGS000138/145, PGS000193, PGS003333, PGS003576-3585, PGS002759, PGS004759/4760, PGS004885), while others are clearly mismatched despite MDD labels (e.g., PGS000907 is muscle pain in fluoxetine takers; PGS000767 is putamen volume; PGS002789 is internalising behavior; PGS003442 is a cognition/BOLD phenotype). Among the endpoint-faithful set, PGS004760 (PRSmixPlus) reports incremental R2=0.024 (95% CI 0.018–0.03) with basic covariates (age, sex, PCs) in European evaluation (n=9,462), which is more directly interpretable as PRS contribution than full-model AUROC/C-index values and avoids the suspiciously high/likely non-comparable R2 reported for PGS000907. It also compares favorably to other endpoint-aligned candidates with lower reported R2 (e.g., PGS000138 full-model R2≈0.018; PGS003333 Nagelkerke R2=0.022; several PGS00357x scores around 0.018–0.026). Remaining limitations: validation sample size for PGS004760 is modest compared with some alternatives, and many candidates report different metric types (full-model vs incremental vs pseudo-R2), so cross-model comparability remains imperfect; therefore confidence is moderate and the overall outcome is sub-optimal rather than high-quality."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000079",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match melanoma PRSs are present (e.g., PGS000079, PGS000118, PGS000732, PGS000743, PGS000766, PGS000790, PGS000813, PGS002246/PGS002247, PGS003430, PGS005208; and multiple PRSweb melanoma models PGS000408–PGS000432). Prioritizing endpoint fidelity, PGS000079 targets incident melanoma with European GWAS/EVAL and reports consistent time-to-event performance signals (full-model AUROC 0.652; C-index 0.663; HR 1.43 [1.36–1.49]) in a very large validation sample (n=392,803). It also represents a coherent, disease-relevant model family (and is reused in PGS000790), while several alternatives are either pan-trait framework-derived (e.g., snpnet PGS001304), restricted-population endpoints (familial melanoma PGS000813; childhood cancer survivors PGS005208), or have much smaller validation (e.g., PGS000743 n=1,035 despite higher full-model AUROC). However, confidence is not high because the best-performing reported AUROC values are full-model metrics that include non-basic UV/phenotypic covariates (behavior/tanning-related variables), and many other direct-match candidates lack directly comparable PRS-only metrics in the provided context, making cross-model performance comparability limited."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005039",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match candidates exist with trait_efo/phenotyping aligned to myocardial infarction, including several UKB snpnet pan-trait scores (PGS001314–PGS001317), a UKB biomarker paper MI score (PGS000710), and a newer multi-ancestry method-comparison family from MVP using PheCode 411.2 (PGS005034–PGS005046). Among the MI-labeled models, PGS005039 shows the strongest reported discrimination in the provided set (full_model C-index=0.7848) with a coherent MI endpoint (\"Myocardial infarction (PheCode 411.2)\") and basic covariates (age, sex, 20 PCs), plus a relatively strong per-SD effect size (OR=1.578, 95% CI 1.50–1.67) and explicit multi-ancestry evaluation (AFR/AMR/EUR each 33%). However, the evidence remains sub-optimal because several near-identical MVP models (PGS005035/037/038/041/044/045/046) have very similar C-index values (~0.782–0.784) and comparable covariates/endpoints, making the top choice only marginally separated; additionally, many candidates report only full-model discrimination rather than clearly isolated PRS-only AUROC/R2, limiting cross-model comparability."
}

{
