{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000903","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 11 candidates are direct matches at the disease-concept level (Parkinson disease). Several, however, are progression/cognitive-decline endpoints (PGS000056, PGS000211) or Parkinson’s disease dementia (PGS000777), which are less endpoint-faithful for a generic “parkinson disease” target than models explicitly phenotyped as Parkinson’s disease status/incidence.

Among the generic Parkinson’s disease endpoint models, PGS000902 and PGS000903 form a coherent disease-focused study family (same Lancet Neurol GWAS meta-analysis, very large training sample n=1,473,098, and shared multi-cohort development including 23andMe/IPDGC/UKB/PPMI/PDBP/HBS). Between them, PGS000903 provides the strongest visible performance support with an AUROC=0.692 (listed in classification_metrics) and an R²=0.054 plus a strong quartile contrast OR=6.25, while keeping covariates to basic demographics/PCs (age, sex, PCs[1-5]). This makes it the best-supported direct-match choice from the available evidence.

Main limitation: the reported AUROC/R² appear to be full-model/unspecified comparability rather than explicitly PGS-only metrics, and competing models (e.g., PGS001774 AUROC=0.8) use heavier, non-comparable covariate packages including family history and multiple risk factors—so cross-model metric comparisons are not clean. This leaves residual uncertainty, hence DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL and only moderate confidence."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005220","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for the target trait (sleep apnea), including multiple generic “Sleep apnea” scores (PGS003204–PGS003218) and several clinically dominant-subtype “Obstructive sleep apnea” scores (PGS003479, PGS003857, PGS003858, PGS005219, PGS005220). Per domain_knowledge, obstructive sleep apnea is the dominant clinical form of sleep apnea and should be treated as a strong direct match when better supported.

Among OSA candidates, PGS005220 (Obstructive sleep apnea, UnadjustedBMI) is preferred because it is disease-focused and multi-cohort in development (FinnGen, MGBB, MVP) with multi-ancestry evaluation listed (European, African American or Afro-Caribbean, East Asian, Hispanic or Latin American) and reports comparatively strong discriminative signal via effect size (OR=1.53, 95% CI 1.39–1.68) alongside an AUROC=0.79. It avoids the explicit BMI-adjusted endpoint framing present in PGS005219 and the MVP-derived genome-wide-significant sparse scores (PGS003857/PGS003858), whose very high AUROC (0.77) is not accompanied here by PRS-only/incremental metrics and is paired with a BMI-adjusted phenotype.

However, overall evidence remains sub-optimal because across these candidates the reported AUROCs are full-model metrics (PGS-only/incremental metrics are missing), and some OSA models use BMI in covariates (raising comparability concerns). Additionally, several ExPRSweb ‘Sleep apnea’ models have near-chance full-model AUROC (~0.49–0.53) and come from a pan-trait repository context, limiting confidence in any single clearly high-quality direct-match PRS choice from the visible metadata alone."}

